==Maltese==

===Etymology===
In pre-modern Maltese, ‘‘h’’ still represented the sound {{IPAchar|[h]}}, as recorded by {{w|Giovanni Pietro Francesco Agius de Soldanis|Agius de Soldanis}} (1750) and {{w|Mikiel Anton Vassalli}} (1796). The modern pronunciation first appeared in the dialect known as {{m|mt||lsien tal-bliet|t=tongues of the cities|pos=referring to the urban areas around the {{w|Grand Harbour}} according to Vassalli}}, and eventually replaced the increasingly archaic {{IPAchar|[h]}} sound in the surrounding regions.

===Pronunciation===
* {{qualifier|phoneme}} {{IPA|mt|/-/|/ː/|/j/|/w/|/ħ/}}

===Letter===
{{letter|mt}}

# {{Latn-def|mt|letter|10}}

====Usage notes====
* In contemporary Maltese, ''h'' remains a true consonant {{IPAchar|[ħ]}} in the following cases:
** in word-final position or when being the last radical of a verb: {{m|mt|ikrah}} {{IPAchar|[ˈɪkraħ]}}, {{m|mt|xebah|jixbhu}} {{IPAchar|[ˈjɪʃpħʊ]}};
** before the negative ending {{m|mt|-x}}: {{m|mt|ra|jarawhx}} {{IPAchar|[jaˈrawħʃ]}};
** in the clusters ''-għh-, -ħh-'', ''-hh-'', which all become {{IPAchar|[ħħ]}}.
* Otherwise it is silent or leaves only a vocalic trace:
** Following and preceding ''a, e, o'' are lengthened if stressed: {{m|mt|hedded}} {{IPAchar|[ˈɛːddɛt]}}, {{m|mt|fehmet}} {{IPAchar|[ˈfɛːmɛt]}}. Other vowels are not affected.
** In intervocalic position it is a glide, {{IPAchar|[j]}} after ''i, ie'', and {{IPAchar|[w]}} after ''u'': {{m|mt||jibniha}} {{IPAchar|[jɪbˈnɪːja]}}, {{m|mt||inħobbuhom}} {{IPAchar|[ɪnħɔbˈbuːwɔm]}}.
** The sequence ''-aho-'' becomes {{IPAchar|[ɔː]}}: {{m|mt||rahom}} {{IPAchar|[rɔːm]}}. The sequence ''-ehi-'' becomes {{IPAchar|[ɛj]}} or {{IPAchar|[ɛˈjiː]}}: {{m|mt|ftehim}} {{IPAchar|[ftɛjm]}}, {{IPAchar|[ftɛˈjiːm]}}.
